827
00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:33.920
Sometimes we dig into the deep stuff, like how I communicate with my dead best friend.

828
00:36:34.220 --> 00:36:37.960
And sometimes we give bad advice based off a TikTok I saw.

829
00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:40.040
And we're not going to apologize for that.

830
00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:41.120
Absolutely not.

831
00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:44.720
You'll laugh, you'll cry, and you'll hire a psychic medium.

832
00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:46.080
Join us, won't you?

833
00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:49.760
Listen to The Deep Dive wherever you get your podcasts from Lemonada Media.

834
00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:59.940
We are back.

835
00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:04.320
We are back from playing Two Lies and a Truth with Joel McHale.

836
00:37:04.320 --> 00:37:05.500
I told you.

837
00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:08.100
I knew when I was like, you never know what you're going to get.

838
00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:12.020
We had no idea he was going to flip the script on us, but that was really fun.

839
00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:15.800
Well, could we get a little around the town?

840
00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:18.180
Around the town.

841
00:37:19.460 --> 00:37:23.820
Our first news item comes from Don T in Litton, British Columbia.

842
00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:24.860
Don says,

843
00:37:25.100 --> 00:37:29.900
Hey, Angela and Jenna, I live in a very small Canadian town along the Fraser River,

844
00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:36.500
and we have a little two-car ferry that I have to go on to get across the river to get to work.

845
00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:42.200
Lately, there have been a lot of large ice chunks floating down the river,

846
00:37:42.200 --> 00:37:44.760
and this caused the paddle to break.

847
00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:49.960
So the ferry shut down for a while, but it's back and running as usual now.

848
00:37:50.260 --> 00:37:51.640
Wow, that's so wild to me.

849
00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:55.540
Just seeing huge chunks of ice floating, that's so wild.

850
00:37:56.060 --> 00:38:00.600
All right, our next around the town is from Stephanie Z in St. Louis, Jenna.

851
00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:01.760
Hi, Stephanie.

852
00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:03.040
Stephanie said,

853
00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:06.580
This week for Valentine's Day, we're seeing something very dear to my heart.

854
00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:09.940
There's a great entertainer, Clownvis Presley,

855
00:38:10.140 --> 00:38:13.600
playing a set of love songs in his style at the City Museum.

856
00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:18.820
I've been seeing his live shows for over 10 years, and he's a local legend.

857
00:38:19.240 --> 00:38:24.160
The first time I visited this beautiful city was in 2021 to see one of his shows.

858
00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:27.660
The year after that, I traveled here solo for another one,

859
00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:31.280
met my partner at a show, and then I moved here in 2022.

860
00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:36.340
We now have a charming little house in South City and five adorable cats.

861
00:38:36.700 --> 00:38:39.620
Neither of us really celebrates this holiday too much,

862
00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:42.840
but it's become our cute slash weird little tradition.

863
00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:46.400
It's also really fun to explore the City Museum at night.

864
00:38:47.300 --> 00:38:49.440
Stephanie, this is so cool.

865
00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:53.140
The last time I was in St. Louis with my family, we went to City Museum.

866
00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:56.160
Angela, it's like no place I've ever been.

867
00:38:56.320 --> 00:38:58.720
It's like five stories and a roof.

868
00:38:58.900 --> 00:39:04.320
It is filled with caves and caverns and crawl spaces

869
00:39:04.320 --> 00:39:11.380
in a giant sculpture of a whale, and you just walk through all of it and climb through all of it.

870
00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:16.040
But then they also have this tank where you can put your hand in and all the fish come over

871
00:39:16.040 --> 00:39:18.020
and like eat the dead skin off your hand.

872
00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:18.840
Oh my goodness.

873
00:39:19.700 --> 00:39:24.980
It's so cool and unique, and I didn't think it could get more unique.

874
00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:29.060
But now I'm hearing that this past Valentine's Day, they had Clownvis Presley.

875
00:39:29.060 --> 00:39:30.220
I had no idea.

876
00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:31.780
This place is really cool.

877
00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:32.780
That is so cool.

878
00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:35.980
Well, our last Around the Town comes from Mandy L.

879
00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:37.200
in Wayne, Pennsylvania.

880
00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:38.540
Mandy says,

881
00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:40.780
I'm just bursting with pride for my niece.

882
00:39:41.100 --> 00:39:42.180
Miss Hayden A.

883
00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:45.060
has been cast as the role of Mrs. Heron

884
00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:48.140
in her school's rendition of Mean Girls Jr.

885
00:39:48.920 --> 00:39:51.520
The role was originated by Miss Anna Gasteyer

886
00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:54.820
and more recently played on the big screen by Miss Jenna Fisher,

887
00:39:55.100 --> 00:39:57.840
wishing many broken legs to Miss Hayden A.

888
00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:02.620
Oh, Hayden, break a leg as Mrs. Heron.

889
00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:03.980
I loved playing that part.

890
00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:04.780
It's a good part.

891
00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:08.080
Well, thank you so much for sending in your Around the Towns.

892
00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:11.620
And now we're going to wrap things up with our office question of the week.

893
00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:13.700
This comes from Craig W.

894
00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:14.640
in Albany.

895
00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:16.500
Craig wants to know,

896
00:40:16.680 --> 00:40:21.000
who was the grown-up in the room when the cast lost it for too long?

897
00:40:21.320 --> 00:40:24.820
How was everyone brought back from contagious giggles?

898
00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:26.480
Oh, Craig.

899
00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:28.120
Craig, this is a good question.

900
00:40:28.460 --> 00:40:29.200
Oh, Craig.

901
00:40:29.480 --> 00:40:30.320
I can tell you what.

902
00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:31.040
It wasn't me.

903
00:40:31.240 --> 00:40:31.920
That's what I can tell you.

904
00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:32.840
It wasn't me.

905
00:40:33.220 --> 00:40:35.740
Who brought us back to being serious?

906
00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:39.320
I can definitely tell you all the people it wasn't.

907
00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:41.200
It was not John Krasinski.

908
00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:42.320
It was not Angela.

909
00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:43.380
It was not me.

910
00:40:43.620 --> 00:40:44.540
It was not Mindy.

911
00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:46.680
It was definitely not Mindy.

912
00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:47.760
It was definitely not Mindy.

913
00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:53.400
Maybe a director would step in and try to wrangle us.

914
00:40:53.860 --> 00:40:59.240
Every once in a while, I know over in accounting, when Brian and I would get the giggles, Oscar would be like, come on, guys.

915
00:40:59.480 --> 00:40:59.980
Come on.

916
00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:06.240
So, sometimes Oscar, but yeah, it was hard.

917
00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:10.360
It was really difficult not to laugh, and we often ruin many takes.

918
00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:12.380
But it was also the crew.

919
00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:20.280
I mean, sometimes you could just see Matt and Randall kind of like shaking with the camera on their shoulder.

920
00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:26.380
Or especially if you had a writer like Jen Salata, she would ruin so many takes herself.

921
00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:29.240
Just as the writer-producer off-camera.

922
00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:30.080
Yeah.

923
00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:33.160
And Greg, too, though, and Paul.

924
00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:33.680
Yeah.

925
00:41:33.820 --> 00:41:41.100
I feel like in terms of people who were off-camera who would break and then get us going, and then it's hard to come back from that,

926
00:41:41.220 --> 00:41:44.560
especially when you've made the off-camera people laugh, too.

927
00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:46.920
Well, that's the biggest win, right?

928
00:41:47.100 --> 00:41:54.800
I remember one time watching our boom operator, Nick Carbone, his handshake as he held the boom because he was laughing.

929
00:41:54.800 --> 00:41:57.340
His, like, shoulders were going, and I was like, yes!

930
00:41:58.520 --> 00:41:59.200
But yeah.

931
00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:01.660
Well, Craig, thank you so much for your question.

932
00:42:01.660 --> 00:42:04.640
It definitely brought a smile to my face remembering all of that.

933
00:42:04.760 --> 00:42:12.760
But I guess our answer is the directors usually had to rein us in, and every once in a while, Oscar, under his breath, would be like, come on, guys.

934
00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:18.520
And maybe Kelly Cantley could get us to pull it together, right?

935
00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:18.840
Yeah.

936
00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:19.700
Or AD.

937
00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:20.240
Yeah.

938
00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:20.960
Maybe Kelly.

939
00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:22.200
I think so.

940
00:42:22.200 --> 00:42:25.460
She would sometimes come in and be like, guys, do we want to make it to lunch?

941
00:42:25.640 --> 00:42:26.420
Let's make it.

942
00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:27.180
Yeah.

943
00:42:27.560 --> 00:42:30.460
Bring up food, and that could get us to become serious.

944
00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:45.160
Lady, do you remember early on, before Nick Carbone joined us, and it might have even been someone who was just on set as, like, an extra set of hands, there was a boom operator.

945
00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:51.740
And we were laughing, and he got really annoyed with us.

946
00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:52.740
Do you remember that?

947
00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:53.960
It was in the kitchen.

948
00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:58.500
We were in the kitchen, and I think I'm thinking of the episode, but I don't want to say what it is.

949
00:42:58.800 --> 00:43:04.940
And we were all busting up laughing, and he got super annoyed, and I thought, man, this is not the job for you.

950
00:43:05.580 --> 00:43:07.340
Like, this is part of it.

951
00:43:07.340 --> 00:43:09.280
Like, we're making comedy here.

952
00:43:09.280 --> 00:43:12.520
We're going to break sometimes, and that's part of the energy.

953
00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:17.620
And I think he didn't find the show as funny as the rest of us.

954
00:43:17.620 --> 00:43:19.140
I don't think he thought it was funny.

955
00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:19.780
Yeah.

956
00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:20.780
I do.

957
00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:21.700
I remember him.

958
00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:23.660
But, yeah, our show wasn't for him.

959
00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:24.300
It wasn't.

960
00:43:24.340 --> 00:43:27.100
He needed to be on a drama, something more serious.

961
00:43:27.340 --> 00:43:28.440
A procedural, yeah.

962
00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:30.100
He wasn't loving it.

963
00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:32.760
But listen, I mean, that comes with the territory on a comedy.

964
00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:39.320
I feel like that's something that I learned, too, is that the atmosphere of making a comedy is comedic.

965
00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:47.340
And if you get too serious or you're just constantly looking at the clock and you're, like, wondering when are we going to get this shot, like,

966
00:43:47.380 --> 00:43:58.900
that's actually a hard role for our first ADs, for Kelly and for Rusty, because they have to leave time for the levity on set to keep the comedy ball in the air.

967
00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:05.620
But you also have to make sure you're getting the shot and that we're breaking in time for lunch and all that kind of stuff.

968
00:44:05.820 --> 00:44:14.960
And so we were really lucky with Kelly and Rusty because they let us have fun, but they were pretty good at, like, reining us in when they needed to as well.

969
00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:20.540
Yeah, they were like your fun aunt and uncle, you know, that let you, like, just let loose.

970
00:44:20.680 --> 00:44:22.380
But then it was like, all right, guys, come on.

971
00:44:22.560 --> 00:44:23.160
Let's go.

972
00:44:23.440 --> 00:44:23.700
Yeah.

973
00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:24.500
Yeah.

974
00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:26.000
You can't stay up too late.

975
00:44:26.380 --> 00:44:27.040
That's right.

976
00:44:28.900 --> 00:44:29.400
Mm-hmm.

977
00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:29.840
Yeah.

978
00:44:30.860 --> 00:44:34.900
Some of the times I laughed so hard, too, was just in the pauses.

979
00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:44.120
I don't know if you remember that, Jenna, but there were so many times when we were in a conference room and Steve as Michael would start improvising.

980
00:44:44.120 --> 00:44:45.760
So we didn't know what he was about to say.

981
00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:48.460
We'd already gotten the scene scripted many times.

982
00:44:48.660 --> 00:44:57.140
And then there'd be one or two moments where Steve as Michael would have a list of things he had to say and he would start improvising that liSt.

983
00:44:57.540 --> 00:45:03.560
And it was the pause before he said the next thing where I would come undone.

984
00:45:03.780 --> 00:45:08.100
The excitement of what he was going to say was so thrilling to me.

985
00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:11.840
And then inevitably, whatever he said would be ridiculously funny.

986
00:45:12.020 --> 00:45:17.520
But it was the pause that got me, like, his long, like, I'm like, oh, my God, I'm out.

987
00:45:17.640 --> 00:45:18.060
I'm out.

988
00:45:18.060 --> 00:45:22.860
All of this reminiscing about this is just, gosh, we laughed a lot.

989
00:45:23.160 --> 00:45:29.480
What a gift that our job was to go somewhere and be around funny people being funny.

990
00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:31.500
There's just nothing like it.

991
00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:32.600
Truly.

992
00:45:33.020 --> 00:45:33.920
We're really lucky.

993
00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:35.100
Yeah.

994
00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:37.400
Oh, you guys, thank you so much.

995
00:45:37.540 --> 00:45:39.140
We hope you enjoyed this episode.

996
00:45:39.140 --> 00:45:43.560
Big thank you to Joel McHale for spending the afternoon with us, being silly.

997
00:45:44.120 --> 00:45:48.620
And thank you to everyone who sends in their chit-chats and around the towns.

998
00:45:48.880 --> 00:45:51.120
We love hearing from you guys so much.

999
00:45:51.220 --> 00:45:53.880
